
// draft
// Int stands in for the iterable's item type, which the problem leaves open; the claims do not
// depend on it
take(n: Int, iterable: Int[]) -> Int[]
	note "return the first n items of the iterable as a list, or all of them when the iterable has fewer than n"
	# If the iterable has at least n items, the output is the iterable's items from position 0 up to
	# but not including position n.

	# If the iterable has fewer than n items, the output is the whole iterable, in its order.
	// example wanted: n 3 over the iterable 0 through 9: more items than n, so only the leading
	// three are kept
	// example wanted: n 10 over the iterable 0, 1, 2: fewer items than n, so the second sentence
	// decides it
	// example wanted: n equal to the iterable's length: the exact threshold, where both sentences
	// apply and must agree
	// example wanted: n 0 over a non-empty iterable: a zero count, decided by the first sentence
	// example wanted: n 2 over an empty iterable: no items at all, decided by the second sentence
	// example wanted: n 2 over the iterable 5, 5, 5: repeated values, so the positions decide which
	// occurrences are kept
	// example wanted: n -1 over a non-empty iterable: the docstring is silent, and islice raises
	// ValueError on a negative count, so the author decides whether this is an error case in a
	// Result or an ordinary input
